Verify the mzML output
Before opening ProtVis, confirm:

the number of .mzML files matches the number of RAW files;
each output file is non-empty and has a plausible file size;
the filename stems are unchanged;
the MSConvert log contains no conversion errors;
the data contain the expected MS levels, especially MS1 and MS2 for DDA proteomics;
every sample was converted with the same settings.

Use SeeMS if you need to open an mzML file and inspect spectra manually.

Windows
Use ProteoWizard/MSConvert when converting supported vendor RAW files on Windows. The Windows tutorial includes:

official ProteoWizard download and installation;
MSConvertGUI workflow;
the annotated ProtVis example screenshot;
output directory and mzML settings;
zlib compression;
Peak Picking / centroiding guidance;
msconvert.exe batch commands;
output verification and ProtVis handoff.

Continue to RAW → mzML on Windows.

Linux
For Thermo RAW on Linux, use ThermoRawFileParser. The Linux tutorial covers:

self-contained Linux release installation;
framework-based execution with .NET 8;
Docker/BioContainers;
single-file and directory conversion;
indexed mzML output;
native peak picking and zlib defaults;
output verification;
direct handoff to ProtVis/Sage on a Linux workstation or server.

Continue to RAW → mzML on Linux.

macOS
For Thermo RAW on macOS, use ThermoRawFileParser. The macOS tutorial covers:

self-contained macOS release installation;
.NET 8 framework-based execution;
Docker option;
macOS executable/security considerations;
single-file and batch conversion;
peak picking and compression;
transfer to another ProtVis/Sage server when needed.

Continue to RAW → mzML on macOS.

ProtVis_dataset schema v4
Schema v4 keeps the physical S4 object compatible while adding a project-wide append-only run system:

analysis_results$v4$runs stores immutable analysis executions;
metadata$result_registry indexes runs;
metadata$workflow stores dependency-aware workflow nodes/edges;
metadata$artifacts indexes generated files;
PSM/peptide assays are registered separately from the active protein matrix;
expression_data always points to the currently activated matrix.

Core preprocessing runs keep complete matrix snapshots. Ordinary analyses keep result tables, statistics, plot data/configuration, parameters, files and upstream dependencies without needlessly duplicating the active matrix.
Useful APIs include:
protvis_result_registry(dataset)
protvis_latest_result(dataset, "normalization")
protvis_result_history(dataset, "normalization")
protvis_result(dataset, run_id)
dataset &lt;- protvis_activate_result(dataset, run_id)
Optional as_QFeatures() / from_QFeatures() helpers connect ProtVis with the Bioconductor QFeatures ecosystem.

Reproduction modes
Several current modules intentionally distinguish normal analysis from archived figure reproduction. In particular:

Enrichment analysis contains the directional KEGG/Figure 3 reproduction workflow with configurable blue-gradient presentation;
GSEA contains Standard GSEA plus a B73-Y12 Figure reproduction mode that can load multiple previous DEP comparisons, focus on Root_VE, and either use the frozen historical target or recalculate from loaded DEP results;
Overview contains an archived Figure 3 UMAP reproduction route.

Use archived modes only when you need historical comparability; use the recommended/default analytical route for new projects.

Built-in Vac14 benchmark
ProtVis includes a published phosphopeptide benchmark based on PXD001057:

protein: AT2G01690.1 (Vac14);
peptide: AT[pS]GVPFSQYK;
phosphosite: Ser3;
precursor charge: 2+.

The example can download/cache its public PRIDE mzIdentML and MGF files and is useful for verifying the local spectrum stack.

The PTM page now contains two tabs:

PTM data overview — upload a CSV/XLSX PTM table, choose peptide/modification settings and generate a summary plot;
PTM spectrum — use the validated Vac14/PXD001057 phosphopeptide spectrum workflow.

