PTM data overview
Upload a PTM table (.csv or .xlsx), then configure:

optional ion additions/removals;
peptide selection;
modification type: Phosphorylation, Ubiquitination, Acetylation, Methylation, Glycosylation;
b/y ion presentation colors.

Click VISUALIZE PTM. The current overview plot summarizes observed numeric/non-empty values by uploaded column and records the uploaded table plus plot configuration as a project run when an active ProtVis_dataset exists.
Treat this view as a data overview rather than a site-localization statistical model.

PTM spectrum: Vac14 benchmark
The built-in spectrum workflow validates a published Arabidopsis phosphopeptide from PXD001057:

protein AT2G01690.1 (Vac14);
modified peptide AT[pS]GVPFSQYK;
phosphosite Ser3;
precursor charge 2+;
run E1R2_SCX5_soluble.

ProtVis can obtain/cache the matching public mzIdentML + MGF evidence, calculate PTM-aware theoretical b/y fragments including relevant neutral losses, match observed peaks and render the evidence table/spectrum.
For general uploaded PSM inspection, use the dedicated PSM Explorer.

Analysis settings
Taxonomic levels and functional levels are detected from the loaded annotation tables rather than being hard-coded to one fixed set. When available, ProtVis prefers Genus for taxonomy and Pathway for function in the initial demo view.
Choose:

taxonomic level;
functional level;
Use relative abundance (default on);
Top categories (default 10).

Click Run Metaproteomics Analysis. When an active dataset exists, the completed run is appended to that project’s schema-v4 history; otherwise ProtVis can create a project state from the abundance table.

Current result panels
The header cards summarize Proteins, Taxa, Functions, Peptides. Result tabs include:

Merged table;
Taxonomy composition;
Taxonomy rank;
Function composition;
Differential categories for taxonomy/function;
Taxon × Function Sankey and heatmap;
Peptide-centric heatmap/table;
Result tables for stored outputs.

Use Merged Protein Table to export the merged table and Current Run RDS to preserve the complete metaproteomics run.

Provenance
Structured provenance can include:

R/ProtVis/package versions;
Sage or FragPipe runtime/version and parameters;
timestamps and run status/errors;
MD5 fingerprints of input/output files;
parent object/run lineage;
generated artifacts and paths.

Portable exports can include provenance.json, provenance.rds, workflow_status.csv, result-registry tables and per-run RDS files.

Dimensionality reduction: archived UMAP reproduction
The current Dimensionality Reduction control is no longer a generic PCA/PCoA/t-SNE/UMAP/NMDS selector. It is an explicit Run UMAP route for the archived Figure 3 reproduction:

transformed pre-KNN intensity matrix (Step4);
five early developmental groups;
random seed 10086;
group colors;
species point shapes;
four biological-region ellipses.

Use this mode when reproducing that historical figure. For a new experiment, interpret UMAP only as an exploratory structure view and preserve the preprocessing/seed used.

Detection filter
Recommended choices include:

detected in ≥N replicates in both groups (default);
detected in ≥N replicates in either group;
any comparison sample (archived behavior);
all proteins.

The default minimum detected replicates is 2.

Additional statistical engines
ProtVis also provides an independent statistical-engine workspace for:

limma — robust/trend empirical-Bayes protein-level model;
DEqMS — uses peptide/PSM evidence counts when available;
proDA — models dropout/missingness and is suited to pre-imputation abundance data;
MSstats — uses native feature-level long-format data when the project contains it.

Availability is checked per engine. If required inputs/packages are missing, the run summary reports the engine as unavailable rather than silently substituting another method.

Replicate-aware rule
For sample names with _1, _2, _3 replicate suffixes, ProtVis groups corresponding replicates per protein:

if at least two replicates are non-zero, missing/zero cells are replaced by half the sum of non-zero replicate values;
if fewer than two replicates are observed, that replicate group becomes missing;
proteins with no positive signal in any replicate are removed in this archived-compatible correction;
duplicated protein IDs are collapsed first, retaining the largest finite intensity per sample;
if no recognizable replicate suffix exists, the validated matrix is returned without forcing this replicate rule.







Note



This is a specific replicate-correction rule, not generic missing-value imputation. The later Imputation module remains a separate operation.

Verify the converted mzML files
Before ProtVis, check file counts and sizes:
find ~/PXD065315_control/mzML -maxdepth 1 -type f -name '*.mzML' -print
ls -lh ~/PXD065315_control/mzML/*.mzML
Confirm:

every RAW file has a matching mzML file;
outputs are non-empty;
filenames match the expected sample names;
the converter exited without errors;
MS1/MS2 spectra are present for DDA data;
all samples used the same conversion settings.

Connect Linux mzML files to ProtVis
If ProtVis is running on the same Linux computer/server:

start ProtVis;
open Project init;
select Raw;
load/upload sample_info containing sample_id and mzml_file;
upload the matching FASTA;
select the Linux mzML directory;
click Check mzML files;
click Project init;
open Search and run Sage.

Example mzML directory:
/home/user/PXD065315_control/mzML
Continue with Raw/mzML + Sage search.

Parser-backed sources
ProtVis currently supports parsers for Proteome Discoverer, DIA-NN, Spectronaut, FragPipe, Skyline, Mascot and OpenMS in addition to MaxQuant.
Examples of recognized structures include:

Proteome Discoverer accession plus Abundance/Area columns;
Skyline long ProteinName/file/Area reports or wide protein tables;
Mascot protein accession plus numeric quantitation such as intensity/emPAI;
OpenMS protein accession plus intensity/abundance/area columns;
DIA-NN/Spectronaut/FragPipe source-specific report layouts.

Parser-backed imports are standardized into a common expression matrix and sample metadata representation while preserving source identity in provenance.

Prerequisites
Complete the Raw route in Project init first:

set a writable working directory;
load sample information with sample_id and mzml_file;
upload the protein FASTA;
select the mzML directory;
click Check LC-MS files;
select Raw;
run Project init.







Important



A Sage project does not need a protein expression matrix before Search. The staging object is intentionally matrix-free until LFQ is produced.

Verify the mzML files
Check the generated files:
find ~/PXD065315_control/mzML -maxdepth 1 -type f -name '*.mzML' -print
ls -lh ~/PXD065315_control/mzML/*.mzML
Confirm:

every expected RAW file produced a matching mzML;
outputs are not empty;
filename stems match the sample metadata;
conversion completed without errors;
expected MS1/MS2 spectra are present for DDA proteomics;
every sample used identical conversion settings.

Continue into ProtVis on macOS
If ProtVis is running locally on the same Mac:

open Project init;
select Raw;
load or upload sample information containing sample_id and mzml_file;
upload the protein FASTA;
select the mzML directory;
click Check mzML files;
resolve any missing/invalid file rows;
click Project init;
open Search and run Sage.

Example directory:
/Users/yourname/PXD065315_control/mzML
Continue with Raw/mzML + Sage search.
